thcal{O}_X(n))\n$$", "MATH_0007": "$S^{(d)}$", "MATH_0008": "$S^{(d')}$", "MATH_0009": "$\\varphi$", "MATH_0010": "$\\beta$", "MATH_0011": "$$\n(\\varphi^*\\mathcal{O}_X(d))^{\\otimes a} =\n\\varphi^*\\mathcal{O}_X(n) =\n(\\varphi^*\\mathcal{O}_X(d'))^{\\otimes a'}\n$$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-converse-construction}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{lemma-converse-construction}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{proof}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\end{proof}"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:e1ae496d72a7bcf863d885975f8833dee0183e393ef286775a524778947d47b4", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:50d5f1531c4b9527f0afc07214bd44abce9575131630aa8ee637ee504f54f8b8", "source_text": "<STRUCT_0001>\nClearly (1) implies (2) and (2) implies (3) by restricting to\nmore divisible degrees and powers of invertible sheaves.\nAlso (3) implies (4) by the uniqueness statement\nin Lemma <REF_0001>.\nThus we have to prove that (4) implies (1). Assume (4),\nin other words <MATH_0001>.\nNote that this implies that we may write\n<MATH_0002> and\n<MATH_0003>.\nMoreover, via these identifications we have that the graded ring\nmaps <MATH_0004> and <MATH_0005> correspond to the restriction of the canonical\ngraded ring map\n<MATH_0006>\nto <MATH_0007> and <MATH_0008> composed with pullback by <MATH_0009>\n(by Lemma <REF_0002> again). Hence taking\n<MATH_0010> to be the isomorphism\n<MATH_0011>\nworks.\n<STRUCT_0002>", "source_text_hash": "sha256:a63fa89a635808ef3f522053ac87cab270b31af79c735ee241179be13b7532dd", "unit_id": "inventory:constructions:01NB:proof/0001"}}}], "input_freeze": {"hash": "sha256:45c389e59852c50770ef95bb81c867023b81e8aca2316393a8433770bec024a7", "path": "translation-data/source-adoption-inputs/p1-27-constructions-d023.json"}, "model_output": {"hash": "sha256:1708d6e152c7efb890a339593873922de1b84c76cddbea482d387a586c5636ed", "path": "docs/evid